<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <xacro:arg name="use_camera" default="true"/>
  <xacro:arg name="use_gazebo" default="true"/>
  <xacro:arg name="prefix" default=""/>

  <!--
    Image size and clip range.

    The previous fixed 424x240 is a D435 *depth* mode, and at the ~0.5 m
    working distance of the calibration rig it puts a 40 mm ArUco marker on
    roughly 22 px. Corner localisation is quantised at about 1.8 mm per pixel
    there, which dominates every other error term in the hand-eye solve.
    848x480 is a native D435 colour mode and halves that to ~0.9 mm/px while
    costing far less render time than 1280x720 at the same field of view.

    The far clip was 1.5 m, which is enough for the marker but clips the table
    and background out of the depth image; 3.0 m keeps the scene intact.
  -->
  <xacro:arg name="camera_width" default="848"/>
  <xacro:arg name="camera_height" default="480"/>

  <!--
    Field of view.

    1.5184 rad = 87 deg, the D435's depth FOV. Kept because this file models a
    D435 and the simulated camera should resemble the sensor it is named after.

    The lens used to be the binding constraint on image coverage, and no longer
    is. The arm's reachable volume is a sphere of radius ~0.26 m about the
    shoulder, so how much of the frame it can occupy is set by how far away the
    camera is: at 0.555 m it subtended only 27.9 deg either side against the
    lens's 43.5 deg, capping the workspace at ~56% of the image width whatever
    the pose planner did, and the target could not be driven to the left or
    right edge of the frame at all. Moving the stand in to 0.380 m raises that
    ceiling to ~90%, which is why the stand moved rather than the lens:

      stand D   workspace spans   cells   edge cells
      0.555 m   56% of width      8/12    6/10
      0.500 m   64% of width      12/12   10/10
      0.400 m   90% of width      12/12   10/10      <- current
      0.378 m   100% of width     12/12   10/10

    A narrower lens remains the other way to buy the same thing, and it would
    additionally shrink the dominant error term. Measured at the OLD 0.555 m
    stand, so the choice can be judged rather than guessed:

      hfov    fx     workspace spans   marker px   cells   edge cells
      87 deg  447    56% of width      ~49         8/12    6/10
      69 deg  617    77% of width      ~69         12/12   10/10
      50 deg  909    100% of width     ~96         12/12   10/10

    (The 69 and 50 deg rows need image_grid at 8x6 rather than 6x5.) 69 deg is
    the D435's colour FOV; 50 deg matches the lens to the workspace but stops
    resembling a D435. Marker pixels matter because the dominant error here is
    a fixed inward bias of about half a pixel per marker edge from anti-aliased
    rendering, which reads as a proportional over-estimate of every distance
    and shrinks with marker PIXELS, not with marker millimetres. Moving the
    stand in already bought most of what a narrower lens would: the marker's
    median span over the sweep went from ~49 px to ~74 px.

    Changing this means changing it in three other places, or the detector
    builds K for one camera while the images come from another, which silently
    scales every translation: BOTH blocks of
    mycobot_calibration/config/handeye_calibration.yaml (the detector uses it
    to build K, the collector to back-project) and
    mycobot_calibration/launch/validate_calibration.launch.py. At 69 deg or
    narrower, image_grid should also go from 6x5 to 8x6 to keep all 12 cells.
  -->
  <xacro:arg name="camera_hfov" default="1.5184"/>
  <xacro:arg name="camera_near_clip" default="0.05"/>
  <xacro:arg name="camera_far_clip" default="3.0"/>
  <xacro:arg name="camera_update_rate" default="5"/>

  <!--
    Where the camera looks.

    The aim has to follow the stand. From the camera's position at
    (0.287, -0.209, 0.281) the workspace centre (0, 0, 0.13) lies 23.16 deg
    down and 55.26 deg to the side, and those are the defaults below: they put
    the optical axis on it exactly.

    That matters for calibration specifically. A hand-eye dataset is supposed
    to put the target across the whole image, edges included, because corner
    localisation and lens distortion both vary across the field and a fit made
    from one patch is only trustworthy in that patch. No pose-planning strategy
    can place the target in image regions the arm cannot reach, so the aim has
    to be right before the poses are chosen.

    An earlier version had these fixed at tilt 25, pan 0, which aimed the
    optical axis along base +Y and 25 deg down while the workspace sat well off
    to the side; it projected to a disc centred on the bottom-left corner of
    the frame, more than half of it outside the image entirely.

    If camera_stand_x/y/z change, these must be recomputed or the workspace
    drifts off centre: at the previous stand position the correct aim was
    tilt 45, pan 37, and using that from the current position would leave the
    optical axis 16.7 deg off the workspace centre.

    The defaults below are the rig this package is calibrated against, chosen
    empirically rather than by aiming at the workspace centre. Aiming at the
    centre scores better on paper (12/12 image cells against 9/12, 45% board
    fill against 41%) but the plate is mounted on the flange facing along the
    tool, so a camera looking steeply down sees it obliquely and close-range
    poses stop detecting altogether. The shallower tilt looks ALONG the arm,
    presents the board closer to square-on, and also conditions the rotation
    axes better (0.78 against 0.63), which is the one degeneracy that no
    number of extra samples can fix.

    The pose planner models the arm as a sphere with no links and no collision
    geometry, so it cannot see self-occlusion and will happily recommend an aim
    that does not detect. Trust a detection over the coverage score.
  -->
  <xacro:arg name="camera_tilt_deg" default="24.45"/>
  <xacro:arg name="camera_pan_deg" default="119.14"/>

  <!--
    Where the stand is, in base_link. Exposed so the camera can be moved
    between runs without editing this file: moving it in the Gazebo GUI does
    not work, because the camera is a link of the robot model welded on by
    fixed joints, and robot_state_publisher would go on publishing TF from the
    URDF regardless. Images would come from the new place while TF insisted on
    the old one, which breaks the ground-truth comparison silently.

    Changing these is a real test of the calibration: it should recover
    whatever pose the camera was actually put at. If you move it, update
    nominal_camera_position in config/handeye_calibration.yaml so the pose
    planner still aims at the right place. That guess costs coverage, not
    accuracy, but a bad one wastes a sweep.

    How far away the stand is, specifically, is the single number that decides
    how much of the frame the calibration target can fill. The arm can only
    bring the plate to within (D - 0.26) m of the camera, and the plate's span
    in pixels is fx * plate_size / distance. At the previous D = 0.555 m the
    plate filled 18% of the frame height at the closest planned pose, well
    under the "board covering the entire image" rule a calibration dataset is
    supposed to satisfy. D = 0.380 m puts it at 90%.

    That 90% comes from the close_up_poses the collector plans explicitly.
    They go as near as the arm can reach, which is (D - 0.26) m, so this
    number sets the fill directly: at D = 0.380 m the arm reaches 0.140 m and
    the 139.5 mm plate spans 90% of the frame height there. Every 10 mm the
    stand moves out costs roughly 6 points of fill.

    Closer still is possible and was deliberately not taken: D = 0.378 m is
    where the workspace exactly fills the 87 deg lens, but it leaves only
    120 mm between the camera and the surface of the arm's reachable sphere.
    In simulation that is free, since the plate carries no collision geometry,
    but it is thin clearance to hand to a physical arm swinging a 140 mm
    board, so the stand sits at 0.400 m with 140 mm of margin instead.
  -->
  <!--
    Camera position RELATIVE TO THE TOP OF THE STAND.

    camera_stand_x/y/z move the stand and the camera together: the pole is a
    child of base_link at (stand_x, stand_y, 0) and the camera is welded to its
    top at height stand_z, so there is no way to reposition the sensor alone
    with those three alone. These three offsets add that, measured from the top
    of the pole, in base_link axes.

    Zero by default, so the geometry is exactly as before unless set.

    The stand is drawn as a 5 mm cylinder with a 0.15 x 0.10 m foot. A small
    offset reads as the camera being on a short bracket. A large one leaves the
    sensor floating in mid-air with the pole somewhere else. That is harmless
    to the physics and to the calibration, which only ever uses
    camera_head_link's TF, but it looks broken on screen and misleads anyone
    reading the scene.

    The 0.01 m on Y is the original stand-to-camera offset and is kept, so
    camera_offset_y is measured from there, not from the pole's axis.
  -->
  <xacro:arg name="camera_offset_x" default="0.0"/>
  <xacro:arg name="camera_offset_y" default="-0.025"/>
  <xacro:arg name="camera_offset_z" default="0.0"/>

  <xacro:arg name="camera_stand_x" default="0.287"/>
  <xacro:arg name="camera_stand_y" default="0.160"/>
  <xacro:arg name="camera_stand_z" default="0.45"/>

  <xacro:if value="${'true' == '$(arg use_camera)' and 'true' == '$(arg use_gazebo)'}">

    <xacro:property name="M_PI" value="3.1415926535897931" />

    <xacro:property name="d435_cam_width" value="0.090"/>
    <xacro:property name="d435_cam_height" value="0.025"/>
    <xacro:property name="d435_cam_depth" value="0.02505"/>

    <xacro:property name="d435_cam_depth_to_infra1_offset" value="0.0"/>
    <xacro:property name="d435_cam_depth_to_infra2_offset" value="-0.050"/>
    <xacro:property name="d435_cam_depth_to_color_offset" value="0.015"/>

    <xacro:property name="camera_tilt_angle_deg" value="$(arg camera_tilt_deg)"/>
    <xacro:property name="camera_pan_angle_deg" value="$(arg camera_pan_deg)"/>

    <link name="torso_link">
      <inertial>
        <origin xyz="0 0 $(arg camera_stand_z)" />
        <mass value="2.5" />
        <inertia ixx="0.8" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyy="0.8" iyz="0.0" izz="0.8" />
      </inertial>
      <visual name="holder">
        <origin xyz="0 0 ${$(arg camera_stand_z)/2}" rpy="0 0 0" />
        <material name="LightGrey">
          <color rgba="0.7 0.7 0.7 1.0"/>
        </material>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder radius="0.005" length="$(arg camera_stand_z)"/>
        </geometry>
      </visual>
      <visual name="base">
        <origin xyz="0 0 0.03" rpy="0 0 0" />
        <material name="LightGrey">
          <color rgba="0.7 0.7 0.7 1.0"/>
        </material>
        <geometry>
          <box size="0.15 0.10 0.06" />
        </geometry>
      </visual>
      <collision name="holder">
        <origin xyz="0 0 ${$(arg camera_stand_z)/2}" rpy="0 0 0" />
        <geometry>
          <cylinder radius="0.005" length="$(arg camera_stand_z)"/>
        </geometry>
      </collision>
      <collision name="base">
        <origin xyz="0 0 0.03" rpy="0 0 0" />
        <geometry>
          <box size="0.15 0.10 0.06" />
        </geometry>
      </collision>
    </link>

    <joint name="$(arg prefix)base_link_to_torso_link" type="fixed">
      <parent link="$(arg prefix)base_link"/>
      <child link="torso_link"/>
      <origin xyz="$(arg camera_stand_x) $(arg camera_stand_y) 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>

    <joint name="camera_head_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="torso_link"/>
      <child link="camera_head_link" />
      <origin xyz="$(arg camera_offset_x) ${0.01 + $(arg camera_offset_y)} ${$(arg camera_stand_z) + $(arg camera_offset_z)}"
              rpy="0.0 ${camera_tilt_angle_deg * M_PI / 180}
                   ${M_PI/2 + camera_pan_angle_deg * M_PI / 180}"/>
    </joint>

    <link name="camera_head_link">
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${M_PI/2} 0.0 ${M_PI/2}"/>
        <geometry>
          <mesh filename="file://$(find mycobot_description)/meshes/d435/visual/d435.stl" />
        </geometry>
        <material name="aluminum">
          <color rgba="0.5 0.5 0.5 1"/>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry>
          <box size="${d435_cam_depth} ${d435_cam_width} ${d435_cam_height}"/>
        </geometry>
      </collision>
      <inertial>
        <mass value="0.072" />
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" />
        <inertia ixx="0.003881243" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyy="0.000498940" iyz="0.0" izz="0.003879257" />
      </inertial>
    </link>

    <joint name="camera_head_depth_joint" type="fixed">
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <parent link="camera_head_link"/>
      <child link="camera_head_depth_frame" />
    </joint>
    <link name="camera_head_depth_frame"/>

    <joint name="camera_head_depth_optical_joint" type="fixed">
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${-M_PI/2} 0 ${-M_PI/2}" />
      <parent link="camera_head_depth_frame" />
      <child link="camera_head_depth_optical_frame" />
    </joint>
    <link name="camera_head_depth_optical_frame"/>

    <joint name="camera_head_infra1_joint" type="fixed">
      <origin xyz="0 ${d435_cam_depth_to_infra1_offset} 0" rpy="0 0 0" />
      <parent link="camera_head_link" />
      <child link="camera_head_infra1_frame" />
    </joint>
    <link name="camera_head_infra1_frame"/>

    <joint name="camera_head_infra1_optical_joint" type="fixed">
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${-M_PI/2} 0 ${-M_PI/2}" />
      <parent link="camera_head_infra1_frame" />
      <child link="camera_head_infra1_optical_frame" />
    </joint>
    <link name="camera_head_infra1_optical_frame"/>

    <joint name="camera_head_infra2_joint" type="fixed">
      <origin xyz="0 ${d435_cam_depth_to_infra2_offset} 0" rpy="0 0 0" />
      <parent link="camera_head_link" />
      <child link="camera_head_infra2_frame" />
    </joint>
    <link name="camera_head_infra2_frame"/>

    <joint name="camera_head_infra2_optical_joint" type="fixed">
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${-M_PI/2} 0 ${-M_PI/2}" />
      <parent link="camera_head_infra2_frame" />
      <child link="camera_head_infra2_optical_frame" />
    </joint>
    <link name="camera_head_infra2_optical_frame"/>

    <joint name="camera_head_color_joint" type="fixed">
      <origin xyz="0 ${d435_cam_depth_to_color_offset} 0" rpy="0 0 0" />
      <parent link="camera_head_link" />
      <child link="camera_head_color_frame" />
    </joint>
    <link name="camera_head_color_frame"/>

    <joint name="camera_head_color_optical_joint" type="fixed">
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${-M_PI/2} 0 ${-M_PI/2}" />
      <parent link="camera_head_color_frame" />
      <child link="camera_head_color_optical_frame" />
    </joint>
    <link name="camera_head_color_optical_frame"/>

    <gazebo reference="camera_head_link">
      <sensor name="camera_head" type="rgbd_camera">
        <camera>
          <horizontal_fov>$(arg camera_hfov)</horizontal_fov>
          <image>
            <width>$(arg camera_width)</width>
            <height>$(arg camera_height)</height>
          </image>
          <clip>
            <near>$(arg camera_near_clip)</near>
            <far>$(arg camera_far_clip)</far>
          </clip>
        </camera>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <gz_frame_id>camera_head_link</gz_frame_id>
        <topic>camera_head</topic>
        <update_rate>$(arg camera_update_rate)</update_rate>
        <visualize>true</visualize>
      </sensor>
    </gazebo>

  </xacro:if>
</robot>